RISC-V extension tracking and related register-allocation fixes, applied after perf-40/41/42.
- Values whose register is already zero-extended (compares, lbu/lhu, small unsigned casts, AND with a
  non-negative operand, srliw by a constant) or sign-extended (W-form arithmetic, loads, long->int casts,
  logic on extended operands) are recognised by checked predicates; their redundant casts are dropped,
  sh*add.uw/add.uw become sh*add/add, and a remaining zero-extension is slli.uw rd,rs,0 rather than
  zext.w (add.uw). Morph and assertion prop keep never-negative int->long casts as sign-extensions and
  bounds-checked array indices use a sign-extending cast, which is usually free on RV64.
- A fixed-point pass at the end of lowering finds INT locals whose every definition is extended (struct
  fields only while the struct is never defined as a whole). Parameters, fields mapped from parameter
  registers and INT call results are covered by an in-place sext.w in the prolog or at the store when
  needed. This replaces perf-28's blanket assumption that every INT local, parameter and call result is
  sign-extended with the checked predicates.
- Fast tail calls no longer contribute a register kill set in LSRA; the kills pushed values live into
  early-exit blocks into callee-saved registers, so every tail-calling method saved and restored them.
- The block-copy unroll gets a second LSRA internal register instead of using RA, so leaf RA elision
  (perf-42) also applies to methods with small struct copies.
- Zero and known-extended INT moves use li/mv instead of sext.w; a zero-extended INT load is one lwu.

